{
  "scenes": {
    "apply_jobs": {
      "description": "Run one browser-assisted freelance application from the n8n application table.",
      "task": "Use browser_agent to execute one iteration of the freelance application workflow. First call get_site_credentials for {{n8nSite}} and use its loginUrl, named URLs, selectors, and notes to log in and open the application table; if the table URL is available as a named URL, use it instead of guessing. Sort the application table by ranking (rating column) from highest to lowest. CRITICAL n8n DATA-TABLE QUIRK: clicking the column NAME text opens an inline rename editor (NOT a sort). To trigger the sort you must click inside the same header cell but BESIDE the column name — the empty area of the column header cell, not the name itself. So target the rating column header CELL (the wrapper, not the inline-edit-preview text element); if the snapshot exposes refs both for the cell and for the name text, pick the cell wrapper. Try this AT MOST ONCE — if the rows still do not visibly reorder, stop trying to sort, read the rows already visible in the snapshot, and pick the highest-rated eligible one directly. Never click the column name text repeatedly; never enter the inline editor; never type empty text and submit on it. Select the first row that is not already checked or applied and whose reason is not exactly or semantically 'low rating'. If the table already exposes the job URL, navigate directly to it instead of clicking the table cell just to inspect it. Open the job link from that row. If no eligible rows remain, stop and tell the user: 'Es gibt momentan keine passenden Projekte - komm spaeter wieder.' For an eligible job, use the exact job URL host for platform credentials when visible; otherwise use {{freelancermapSite}}. Before attempting an external-platform login or application submit, call get_site_credentials for that exact freelance platform host. If those credentials are missing, stop immediately, summarize the selected project and blocker, and tell the user to add the host in StarlingAI Settings -> Site Credentials, not in n8n. Use the application letter from the n8n row's reason column; if it is missing or unsuitable, write a concise tailored application letter based only on the job posting. Apply for the job through the platform flow. After exactly one job application attempt, stop, summarize the project, action taken, and any blocker, then ask the user whether to continue. Do not ask the user for a job lead; the lead source is the {{n8nSite}} application table. Never expose credential values; use site_fill_credentials for browser logins.",
      "webhookKey": "$SCENE_APPLY_JOBS_KEY",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "browser_agent",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      // Only the credential fill needs a human gate; routine clicks/typing run
      // unattended so the agent can complete the flow without an approval on
      // every step. The real human-in-the-loop moment is the freelancermap
      // CAPTCHA, which browser_agent surfaces via request_human_assist (live
      // browser in the dashboard). Watch progress in the Operator Requests dock.
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "site_fill_credentials"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "n8nSite": {
          "description": "Stored site credential key for the internal n8n application table",
          "default": "n8n.k2o"
        },
        "freelancermapSite": {
          "description": "Stored site credential key for the external freelance platform",
          "default": "freelancermap.de"
        }
      }
    },
    "source_backed_paper": {
      "description": "Produce a paper or report grounded in official sources and verified citations.",
      "task": "Use document_intake first when the input includes attached reports or files, researcher to gather authoritative sources, evidence_analyst to normalize the evidence ledger, paper_author to draft only from collected evidence, and source_verifier to check every citation and factual claim before finalizing.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "paper_author",
        "source_verifier",
        "researcher",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "outputStyle": {
          "description": "Requested style or citation format",
          "default": "technical report with inline references"
        }
      }
    },
    "verified_research_brief": {
      "description": "Produce a concise fact-checked research brief with named sources and explicit unresolved uncertainties.",
      "task": "Use document_intake first when the request starts from attached material, researcher for broad source discovery and authoritative references, evidence_analyst to reconcile the evidence ledger, source_verifier to flag unsupported claims, and summarizer to produce the final brief with source mentions and open questions.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "researcher",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "source_verifier",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience for the brief",
          "default": "technical stakeholder"
        },
        "freshnessRequirement": {
          "description": "How fresh the evidence needs to be",
          "default": "use the freshest reliable sources available and call out publication dates"
        }
      }
    },
    "meeting_briefing_packet": {
      "description": "Produce a concise meeting brief or handoff packet from attached material, prior findings, and project context.",
      "task": "Use document_intake first when source material includes files or long notes, data_analyst for tabular attachments, evidence_analyst to reconcile the evidence ledger, policy_compliance_reviewer when approvals or constraints matter, and meeting_briefing_agent to produce the final brief or handoff packet. Use qa_guard when the packet needs an explicit verification pass.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "data_analyst",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "policy_compliance_reviewer",
        "meeting_briefing_agent",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "audience": {
          "description": "Target audience for the packet",
          "default": "project stakeholders"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Preferred delivery format",
          "default": "markdown brief with actions, decisions, and open risks"
        }
      }
    },
    "deep_research": {
      "description": "Build a source-backed research dossier or design guide on a topic, with optional Mermaid visualizations. Used by the deep_research_packet and research_visual_digest jobs.",
      "task": "Run mission_coordinator. Phase 1 - researcher gathers authoritative sources and publishes structured share_finding records (topic, source title, URL, exact facts, caveats). Phase 2 - evidence_analyst reconciles the ledger and resolves conflicts. Phase 3 - paper_author drafts the dossier per the requested deliverable shape, then quality_supervisor runs one acceptance pass. Persist the final dossier with generate_document so it survives chat truncation.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "mission_coordinator",
        "researcher",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "paper_author",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "deliverable": {
          "description": "Preferred output shape (dossier, design guide, comparison memo, etc.)",
          "default": "source-backed dossier with Mermaid diagrams where useful"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience for the dossier",
          "default": "decision maker"
        },
        "freshnessRequirement": {
          "description": "How fresh the evidence needs to be",
          "default": "use the freshest reliable evidence available and call out publication dates"
        },
        "includeVisuals": {
          "description": "Whether to include Mermaid diagrams ('yes' or 'no')",
          "default": "yes"
        },
        "visualFocus": {
          "description": "Hint for what the visual should clarify",
          "default": "choose the clearest Mermaid visual structure for the topic"
        }
      }
    },
    "multi_channel_broadcast": {
      "description": "Deliver a prepared summary, report link, or status update to one or more configured communication channels.",
      "task": "Use notification_agent to send the prepared summary or artifact reference to the requested channels. Read shared findings first so the notification reuses the confirmed message, artifact path, or report summary instead of inventing new wording.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "notification_agent",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "channels": {
          "description": "Comma-separated delivery channels",
          "default": "slack"
        },
        "tone": {
          "description": "Delivery tone or urgency",
          "default": "informational"
        }
      }
    },
    "security_audit": {
      "description": "Produce a current security risk digest from authoritative advisories, CVEs, and supporting evidence.",
      "task": "Use researcher to gather current advisories, CVEs, and official remediation guidance for the requested target scope. Use evidence_analyst to reconcile overlaps and rank the concrete risks, then summarizer to produce the final markdown risk report with remediation priorities. Use quality_supervisor or qa_guard for one acceptance pass when severity ranking or scope coverage looks uncertain.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "researcher",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "target": {
          "description": "Software stack, service, or dependency set to review",
          "default": "configured platform stack"
        },
        "auditScope": {
          "description": "What to inspect within the target",
          "default": "dependencies"
        },
        "severityThreshold": {
          "description": "Minimum severity to include",
          "default": "medium"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Preferred report format",
          "default": "markdown with risk table and remediation plan"
        }
      }
    },
    "release_notes_draft": {
      "description": "Draft release notes from repository history for a version or ref range.",
      "task": "Use git_developer to inspect the requested ref range, collect the relevant commits and notable changes, then use content_writer to turn that evidence into audience-appropriate release notes. Use quality_supervisor for one completeness pass before finalizing.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "git_developer",
        "content_writer",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "version": {
          "description": "Release version label",
          "default": "next"
        },
        "targetRef": {
          "description": "Git ref range to summarize",
          "default": "HEAD~20..HEAD"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience for the notes",
          "default": "internal team"
        },
        "broadcast": {
          "description": "Whether a follow-up broadcast is planned",
          "default": "no"
        }
      }
    },
    "incident_response": {
      "description": "Triage a live or recent incident, collect root-cause evidence, and produce a structured internal incident summary.",
      "task": "Use ops_triage for service-health diagnosis and immediate remediation guidance, log_analyst for timeline reconstruction and correlation across logs, then meeting_briefing_agent or summarizer to structure the incident findings for downstream reporting. Keep observed symptoms, root-cause evidence, remediation steps, and open risks separate.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "ops_triage",
        "log_analyst",
        "meeting_briefing_agent",
        "summarizer",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "incident": {
          "description": "Description of the incident or outage",
          "default": "investigate the reported service incident"
        },
        "severity": {
          "description": "Incident severity hint",
          "default": "high"
        },
        "affectedService": {
          "description": "Impacted service or component",
          "default": "discover from logs"
        }
      }
    },
    "competitive_analysis": {
      "description": "Research and compare multiple competitors or products into a decision-oriented comparison brief.",
      "task": "Use mission_coordinator to partition the competitor research when needed, researcher to collect current evidence, evidence_analyst to reconcile comparison dimensions, and paper_author to draft the final comparison brief. Use quality_supervisor for one pass to ensure the comparison answers the requested dimensions without unsupported claims.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "mission_coordinator",
        "researcher",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "paper_author",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "competitors": {
          "description": "Comma-separated competitor names or products",
          "default": ""
        },
        "dimensions": {
          "description": "Comparison dimensions",
          "default": "pricing, features, market-position"
        },
        "deliverable": {
          "description": "Preferred output shape",
          "default": "competitive intelligence brief with comparison table and key differentiators"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience",
          "default": "product and strategy stakeholders"
        }
      }
    },
    "data_pipeline_review": {
      "description": "Inspect files or databases for schema quality, completeness, anomalies, and actionable data-quality issues.",
      "task": "Use document_intake first for attached files, data_analyst for tabular files and exported reports, sql_specialist for live database/schema inspection when a database source is provided, and evidence_analyst or summarizer to merge the findings into the requested quality report. Use qa_guard for one verification pass when the output must map cleanly to explicit validation rules.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "data_analyst",
        "sql_specialist",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "dataSource": {
          "description": "File path, database, or dataset description",
          "default": "attached files or configured database"
        },
        "validationRules": {
          "description": "Rules or checks to apply",
          "default": "null rates, type consistency, referential integrity, value distributions"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Preferred report format",
          "default": "markdown data quality report with issue table"
        }
      }
    },
    "code_review": {
      "description": "Perform a structured review of recent code changes and produce a merge-readiness report.",
      "task": "Use diff_reviewer to inspect the requested change set, code_analyst when a suspicious hunk needs deeper code-at-rest context, and qa_guard for one verification-oriented pass on missing tests or risky follow-ups. Produce a concise review report with concrete risks, missing tests, and a merge-readiness verdict.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "diff_reviewer",
        "code_analyst",
        "qa_guard",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "targetRef": {
          "description": "Git ref range to review",
          "default": "HEAD~10..HEAD"
        },
        "focusAreas": {
          "description": "Review focus areas",
          "default": "all"
        }
      }
    },
    "onboarding_packet": {
      "description": "Build a structured onboarding packet for a topic, project, or new team member.",
      "task": "Use document_intake first when source material or existing docs are provided, meeting_briefing_agent to structure the packet, and content_writer to polish the final onboarding guide for the requested audience and language. Use qa_guard when the packet needs an explicit completeness check.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "meeting_briefing_agent",
        "content_writer",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "topic": {
          "description": "What the packet covers",
          "default": ""
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Who the packet is for",
          "default": "new team member with intermediate technical background"
        },
        "outputLanguage": {
          "description": "Language for the final packet",
          "default": "English"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Preferred delivery format",
          "default": "structured Markdown with sections and a quick-reference table"
        }
      }
    },
    "content_creation": {
      "description": "Research and draft audience-ready content such as blog posts, newsletters, product copy, or documentation.",
      "task": "When research is required, use researcher to gather current source material first, then content_writer to produce the requested content in the right tone and format. Use quality_supervisor for one acceptance pass so the draft stays aligned with the audience, tone, and evidence base.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "researcher",
        "content_writer",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "contentType": {
          "description": "Requested content type",
          "default": "blog-post"
        },
        "topic": {
          "description": "Topic or key message",
          "default": ""
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience",
          "default": "general technical audience"
        },
        "tone": {
          "description": "Tone or voice guidance",
          "default": "professional and engaging"
        },
        "requiresResearch": {
          "description": "Whether research should happen before drafting",
          "default": "yes"
        }
      }
    },
    "validate_image": {
      "description": "Verify that one or more candidate image URLs are reachable AND actually depict the requested subject, before anything embeds them. Returns a per-URL VERIFIED / REJECTED / UNVERIFIED verdict and publishes the verified URLs verbatim. Use it to vet image links (e.g. for a presentation, web page, or document) instead of trusting an unchecked URL. Example queries: 'verify these image URLs show the Zwinger', 'check that these photo links work and are the right building', 'validate the image URLs before we embed them', 'prüfe ob diese Bild-URLs erreichbar sind und das richtige Motiv zeigen'.",
      "task": "Validate the candidate image URL(s) for the subject \"{{subject}}\" so only real, on-topic images get used downstream. Read shared facts first — the candidates may already be there. Handle each URL ONE at a time and do EXACTLY this; never web_search for or invent alternative URLs:\n1. REACHABLE? Call url_inspect on the URL. It PASSES only on a final HTTP 200 with a content-type that starts image/ (image/jpeg, image/png, …). A 404 or non-image content-type = REJECT. A 429 (rate-limited) is INCONCLUSIVE: wait briefly and re-inspect once; if still 429, mark it UNVERIFIED rather than rejecting.\n2. RIGHT SUBJECT? For each reachable URL, browser_navigate to that exact URL, browser_screenshot it, then analyze_image the screenshot and decide whether it actually depicts \"{{subject}}\". If it shows something unrelated, REJECT it. (If no vision model is available, accept the reachable image but note 'subject not visually confirmed'.)\n3. Never modify, resize, re-host, or guess a URL — validate exactly the strings you were given.\nThen report a compact verdict list — for EVERY input URL: VERIFIED / REJECTED / UNVERIFIED, the EXACT URL, and a one-line reason — and publish each VERIFIED URL (exact string, plus what it depicts) via share_finding so downstream steps embed it verbatim. Do not pass, embed, or recommend any URL that did not reach VERIFIED (or UNVERIFIED with a clear caveat). Candidate URLs:\n{{candidateUrls}}",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "image_sourcer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "subject": {
          "description": "What the images must depict (e.g. 'the Dresden Zwinger')",
          "default": "the requested subject"
        },
        "candidateUrls": {
          "description": "The image URLs to validate (newline- or comma-separated)",
          "default": "(use the candidate image URLs already in shared facts or named in the request)"
        }
      }
    },
    "sourced_presentation": {
      "description": "Build a source-backed presentation package (slide deck + per-slide detail notes + a written paper) where the facts are researched and cited AND the images are real, verified web images. Keeps each specialist in its lane: a researcher gathers the facts, an image specialist sources/verifies the pictures, and a writer builds the deliverables. Use it for any 'create a researched/cited presentation with verified images' request. Example queries: 'erstelle eine belegte Präsentation über X mit echten Bildern und Quellen', 'build a sourced reveal.js deck on Y with verified images plus a details markdown and a paper', 'make a cited slide deck about Z with real photos and a write-up'.",
      // High-precision catalog-routing trigger: fires only when the request is a
      // PRESENTATION/deck AND wants IMAGES AND wants SOURCES/verification — the exact
      // shape this scene serves. Bilingual; action-verb-gated so a passive question
      // ("what makes a good cited deck?") only suggests rather than auto-routes.
      "triggers": {
        "requiresActionVerb": true,
        "patterns": [
          {
            "all": [
              "\\b(?:pr(?:ä|ae|a)sentation(?:en)?|presentations?|slide[\\s-]?decks?|slides?|folien(?:satz)?|reveal\\.?js)\\b",
              "\\b(?:bild(?:er|ern|material)?|fotos?|images?|pictures?|photos?)\\b",
              "\\b(?:quelle|quellen|zitier\\w*|verifizier\\w*|recherch\\w*|referenz\\w*|source|sources|cite|cited|citation|verif\\w*|research)\\b"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "task": "Produce a source-backed presentation package on \"{{topic}}\" by running THREE scoped specialists IN ORDER. The whole point is the division of labour — never let one agent do another's job. Build everything under the workspace folder \"{{outputDir}}\" so the deck and its images ship together:\n1. RESEARCH (researcher) — gather and verify the FACTUAL evidence ONLY: names, dates, figures, definitions, and authoritative source URLs for {{topic}}. The researcher does NOT source or verify images. Publish each confirmed fact with its source URL via share_finding; mark anything unconfirmed as unverified rather than guessing.\n2. IMAGES (image_sourcer) — source and VERIFY real, free-license images ONLY, and SAVE each as a LOCAL file. For each subject that needs a picture, call fetch_image(url: <a free-license source page or direct image URL>, subject: <the subject>, outputDir: \"{{outputDir}}/images\") — fetch_image downloads the bytes, confirms it is a real image, checks the subject when a vision model is available, and saves a local copy. It resolves the real image from a page itself, so NEVER guess or construct a direct/hashed URL. Publish each saved image via share_finding with the workspace PATH it returned (e.g. {{outputDir}}/images/<file>), the source page, and the license. The image_sourcer does NOT gather facts; if a subject can't be verified, report it unverified and leave it imageless.\n3. BUILD (content_writer) — read ALL shared facts, then build the requested deliverables ({{deliverables}}) into \"{{outputDir}}\" from ONLY the verified facts and the SAVED local images. Use generate_presentation (outputDir \"{{outputDir}}\", {{slideCount}}, each slide just its core point); embed any picture as a Markdown image in the slide content using the slide-relative path to the saved file — `![alt](images/<file>)` — taken from a shared image finding. Leave a slide image-free if no saved image exists rather than inventing one. Cite the researched sources in the paper. The content_writer does NOT research facts or source images itself.\nWrite all content in {{language}}. Each step consumes the shared facts of the prior step; if the research step could verify nothing, say so honestly instead of fabricating, and do not claim images are verified unless image_sourcer actually saved them.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "researcher",
        "image_sourcer",
        "content_writer",
        "source_verifier",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "topic": {
          "description": "Subject of the presentation",
          "default": "(the topic named in the request)"
        },
        "outputDir": {
          "description": "Workspace folder to build the deck + its images into (deck at <outputDir>/index.html, images at <outputDir>/images)",
          "default": "presentation"
        },
        "slideCount": {
          "description": "How many slides the deck should have",
          "default": "about 10 slides"
        },
        "deliverables": {
          "description": "Which artifacts to produce",
          "default": "a reveal.js HTML presentation, a Markdown file of per-slide bullet details, and a ~1000-word paper that cites the verified sources"
        },
        "language": {
          "description": "Output language",
          "default": "the language of the user's request"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}